  "explain": {
   "text": "One place on the line can have many names. Cut every eighth in two and 3/8 becomes 6/16, with the mark still at 0.375. Multiply top and bottom by the same number and the fraction keeps its place.[^1] Names at the same point are [[equivalent fractions]].[^2]\n\nThe trap is adding instead: 3/8 to 4/9, one more on top and bottom. That moves the mark from 0.375 to about 0.444.\n\n**Same multiplier on top and bottom renames a fraction without moving it: 3/8 = 6/16 = 12/32.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "2.3 kW and 2300 W are the same load: a smaller unit, a thousand times the count. Renaming a fraction works the same way.",
   "breaks": "Units step in fixed thousands; a fraction can be cut by any whole number you like."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** do the same to top and bottom: multiply or divide, never add.\n\n**Worked example:** a hole sits 5/8 inch in; the tape shows sixteenths. 16 ÷ 8 = 2, so 5/8 = 10/16.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Adding: 3/8 to 4/9 moves the mark.\n- Changing only the bottom: 3/16 is half of 3/8."
  },
